 and  to really deal with this issue there  needs to be a  all encompassing multifaceted solution  and there's a couple different problems  here right number one is an issue of  tactics the pro-gun right is correct  that if you just limit the types of guns  or ammo alone you're not going to fix  the broader gun violence problem in the  United States if you are on the pro-gun  right you're wrong if you say we should  only be looking at mental health it's  not just a mental health issue the left  likewise is wrong if they believe that  background checks and banning assault  weapons is the panacea to solve this  problem this is about availability of  guns lack of background checks and  required training it is about mental  health but most importantly and sort of  more broadly this is about the toxic  relationship that exists in the United  States between too many Americans and  their guns where people see firearms as  tools to solve problems this means tools  to solve interpersonal problems tools to  solve political disagreements and  disputes that's the part that really has  to be changed and I don't actually know  how you change that but in the meantime  there are other things we can do last  thing I want to say about this there's a  growing movement on the Internet mostly  that like to say that they are about  principles and ideas and they say we  don't want to get mixed up so much in  the day-to-day politics and policy  because the principles are what matter  if you can't identify your principles  then you're gonna be completely clueless  about what are the actual policies you  should support or oppose the problem  with that and I've been working on this  a little bit I've been talking to one of  our longtime viewers and friends a Greg  Stephens about this and and some other  people the problem is that unless you  realize that no set of principles will  be all encompassing you will falsely be  lulled into this idea that if you can  just come up with some hard and fast  principles you never will actually have  to do the work of thinking through  complicated situations and that relates  to guns how it relates to guns because  if you take the position that we have a  Second Amendment and we do not need nor  does the Constitution  for common-sense gun safety regulation  you don't actually have to do the tough  work of figuring out what is  specifically going on in the US when it  comes to guns how would a combination of  mental health and restrictions on  firearms and training and background  checks how would all of those things  together impact the problem that we have  you can just do the easy thing which is  cite a principle based on a multi  hundred-year-old document and your  particular interpretation of that Second  Amendment and say listen I'm a  constitutionalist and this is what the  Constitution says that's really what  matters and that is it is a dangerous  movement that we can apply to a lot of  places this is not me going on some kind  of like postmodern rant where nothing  has meaning and everything is equally  valid and there's no objective reality  that's not what I'm saying what I am  Telling You is that any movement that's  looking for sort of universal principles  that will apply in every situation and  sort of excuse you from having to  actually look at the numbers I presented  to you here about gun violence and say  we've actually got to go beyond just our  principles and ideals and figure out  solutions it is actually a cop out and I  would encourage a lot of the people that  are being bamboozled by this internet  movement of let's just list our  principles and debate ideas you're  falling for something that will not  actually get get us to real-world  solutions and that's a real problem  discuss this with us on our reddit  discussion forum at david pakman.com  slash our IDI di t today's program is  sponsored in part by movement you guys  have heard me talk about movement you  know about those two college dr